      "math_academy_id": "q-129206",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-6$, $4$, and $1$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-6+4+1=-1.$",
